{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1792197000000-architect-yang-ts-php-designs.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md | sed -n '100,116p' && sed -n '255,282p' docs/design/yin.vm.typecheck.md && date -u '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S GMT' && date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S %z'\"","aggregated_output":"   100\t\n   101\tContext map as Python's (`:lower` self-reference, `:loop {:brk :cont}`, `:ret`, `:this`, `:gen`, `:async`, `:class`, `:scope`), name supply `%<prefix><cst-id>` (`%` cannot begin a JS identifier either), `:yang/site` marks on loop and function lambdas.\n   102\t\n   103\t**Scope analysis (`yang.typescript.antlr.scope`)**: hoisting is the whole job. Per function scope: `var` and function declarations hoisted to function top (cells allocated `:js/undefined`, functions initialized first); per block: `let`/`const`/`class` cells allocated `:js/tdz`; per-iteration copies for `for (let ...)` (a fresh cell per iteration, closed over correctly); `arguments` object only in non-arrow functions that mention it; `this` lexical for arrows (a parameter threaded from the enclosing non-arrow function); strict mode is always on (modules) and script-goal sloppy semantics are out of scope except where listed (`:js/sloppy` refused: `with`, implicit globals, duplicate params). Every binding is a cell (mob D5 holds: closures and continuations).\n   104\t\n   105\t| Group | Arms | Lowering |\n   106\t|---|---|---|\n   107\t| Expressions | literals, template literals (`js/to-string` per substitution, tagged templates in T5), identifiers, member/index access, optional chaining (`js/get` with short-circuit through `if` on nullish), `new`, calls (`js/call f this args`), spread in calls and arrays, `typeof`/`void`/`delete`/`in`/`instanceof`, arithmetic/bitwise/logical/`??`, comparison (`js/loose-eq`, `js/strict-eq`, `js/compare` with ToPrimitive), assignment and compound assignment (receiver and key evaluated once, `%o`/`%k`), `++`/`--`, comma, conditional, arrow and function expressions, class expressions, object literals (shorthand, computed keys, methods, getters/setters, spread), array literals (holes), regex literals (host-effect-free: refused in Phase 1, see A7) | ordinary `:application` of `js/*` |\n   108\t| Destructuring | array/object patterns in declarations, params, assignment, defaults, rest | `js/iter-destructure`, `js/get` chains |\n   109\t| Statements | var/let/const, if, for, for-in (own enumerable string keys in spec order, snapshot at start), for-of (iterator protocol), while/do, switch (strict-eq chain with fallthrough via sequencing), break/continue (labelled), return, throw, try/catch/finally (optional catch binding), block, labelled, empty, expression | loops: self-applied lambda `:loop` site; escapes: `js/call-ec` with `unwind-to`; `finally`: `js/try-finally` |\n   110\t| Functions | declarations, defaults, rest, `arguments`, `this` binding modes (strict `this` = undefined when called bare), `new.target` | `js/make-function name code this-mode proto-slot` (a cell: functions are objects) |\n   111\t| Classes | `class`, `extends`, `constructor`, `super()` and `super.m`, methods, static, getters/setters, fields (public, `#private`, static), static blocks | `js/make-class`: constructor function cell plus prototype cell; `extends null`; derived constructors must call `super` before `this` (TDZ cell on `this`) |\n   112\t| Generators | `function*`, `yield`, `yield*` | the landed continuation machinery: `js/make-generator`, `js/gen-switch`, `js/yield-raw`; iterator protocol objects (`next`/`return`/`throw`) over the same cell as 8.5.3; same flag-cell pattern, fixed retention (ruling 7) accepted |\n   113\t| Async | `async function`, `await`, `for await`, async generators | T6: `await` is a suspension via the generator core; the job queue is prelude (A9) |\n   114\t| Modules | `import`/`export` forms, `import()` with a literal specifier, `import.meta` (refused) | A8 |\n   115\t| Type layer | every type-only rule in `consumed-rules`; `as`/`satisfies`/`!`/casts return their operand; `enum`, `namespace`, parameter properties as A3 | T8, T9 |\n   116\t\n\n---\n\n## 4. The checker contract\n\nThe observer is parameterised by one pure function:\n\n    check : packet claims -> verdict-body\n\n`packet` is one tree packet; `claims` is the set of claim rows whose\n(projected) occurrence lies in that packet; `verdict-body` is the outcome,\nevidence (`claim-refs`), derived claims, and defects of section 2.3. The\nfunction reads no host clock, stream, registry, or mutable state, and it is\ntotal: an unsupported tag or a malformed claim is a `:refused` verdict with a\nnamed rule, never a throw. Throws are reserved for defects and terminal\nstream outcomes, as in the expander.\n\nBecause `check` is pure and its inputs are rows, it may itself be a `yin.vm`\nprogram run under a step budget in a child VM -- the one general facility\n`yin.vm.macro.md` already requires for macros. Nothing here needs a second\nsandbox.\n\nThis document fixes no type language. The first checker (section 9, T1) is\na claims-consistency checker: it derives nothing and only refuses a packet\nwhose declared claims contradict each other at a meeting point (an\napplication whose operator has a declared arity that its operand count\nviolates; a binder declared twice with unequal types). That checker is\nenough to prove the topology and the media; it is not a type system, and it\n2026-10-17 07:48:21 GMT\n2026-10-17 14:48:21 +0700\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-17 07:48:21 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-17 14:48:21 +0700\n\nReviewed the complete revision against HEAD `5f8963093631ab447840cd6a041d83fadccf3999`; tracked files match HEAD. No files edited. Verification used source inspection and an in-memory Node probe.\n\nThe original PHP unset fixture now yields `1` under the ruled semantics. SCC commitment fixes the previous synchronous evaluation failure, and the owning-root table fixes the alias-root mistake. However, the lifecycle rules still have gaps:\n\n1. **[P1] PHP releases holders that copies never acquired.**  \n   [Revision:40–43](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792214000000-architect-ts-php-r3.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:40) excludes copied markers from holder counts but unconditionally releases a holder when such a slot is removed:\n   ```php\n   $a = [1]; $r =& $a[0];\n   $b = $a;\n   unset($b[0]);\n   $a[0] = 2;\n   echo $r; // PHP: 2\n   ```\n   The ruled count starts at 2, remains 2 on copy, then drops to 1 on removal. Writing `$a[0]` consequently demotes instead of updating `$r`, producing `1`. PHP separation preserves the surviving reference holders. [PHP 8.3 duplication implementation](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/php/php-src/PHP-8.3/Zend/zend_hash.c).\n\n   This has a live variable holder and no scope exit, so neither documented departure covers it. Demoting a copied count-one marker also “releases” a cell still referenced by the source array. Define ownership across copy, removal and demotion so releases correspond to acquired holders; otherwise the count cannot safely govern writes.\n\n2. **[P1] Dynamic import cannot use the static cycle shortcut.**  \n   [Revision:147](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792214000000-architect-ts-php-r3.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:147) permits an import into an evaluating component to synchronously treat `:evaluating` as the cycle case. That shortcut establishes neither successful completion nor the eventual import outcome.\n\n   For a module that starts `import(\"./self\")` and then throws `e`, the pending self-import must reject with that same `e`. The Node probe confirmed this. Specify scheduling and promise settlement against the component’s completion; neither synchronous waiting nor early success is sufficient. This requirement remains without top-level await. [ES2022 module evaluation](https://tc39.es/ecma262/2022/multipage/ecmascript-language-scripts-and-modules.html#sec-moduleevaluation).\n\n3. **[P1] Late-claim rechecking lacks evidence replay.**  \n   [Revision:107](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792214000000-architect-ts-php-r3.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:107) says late `.d.ts` claims bind on the next packet carrying the root. But the governing [observer lifecycle](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.typecheck.md:189) discards the packet’s claim index after its verdict and advances its claims cursor.\n\n   Replaying code with a later watermark therefore does not, by itself, restore the original source claims. The proposed `x: number` versus late `x: string` fixture needs both claims together. Define complete evidence replay—through a fresh cursor, an explicitly supplied snapshot, or another bounded mechanism—and test publication, first verdict, late declaration, then recheck.\n\n4. **[P2] T10’s guest-call arity fixture still exceeds the generic T1 checker contract.**  \n   [Revision:118](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792214000000-architect-ts-php-r3.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:118) expects refusal of `f(1, 2)`. Yet retained [lowering rules](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792197000000-architect-yang-ts-php-designs.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md:107) emit `js/call f this args`, with guest functions represented as objects and bindings as cells. The UAST application’s operator is `js/call`, not the annotated binder `f`.\n\n   Specify the guest-aware analysis connecting that application to `f` and its argument list, or use the conflicting-declaration fixture for frontend acceptance and a direct UAST application for generic checker arity.\n\nThe three instances can remain separate drivers over cells and publication datoms. Sharing lifecycle terminology does not resolve these ownership, completion and evidence-retention requirements.\n\nVerdict: REQUEST CHANGES"}}
